sam: Quick check — our Rust SDK is panicking when streaming SSE from hosted API if the connection gets slow. Appears to be partial JSON slices across chunks. Anyone seen this?

anya: yep, seen that before. SSE -> you get `data:` frames that can split JSON mid-object. Need to reassemble `data:` lines into a buffer and only call the JSON parser once you detect end-of-event (\n\n).

miguel: do we already use a streaming JSON parser? `serde_json` will panic if you feed it half a token. Use `serde_json::Deserializer` incremental API or `simd-json`'s incremental parser.

sam: we currently just `collect::<Vec<u8>>()` per chunk and try `serde_json::from_slice` — yeah that fails on partial. Any code pattern recommended for Rust + SSE?

anya: rough pattern: accumulate bytes for an event, strip the leading `data: ` prefixes (multiple lines possible), join them with `\n`, then try parse. Pseudocode:```rust
let mut buf = BytesMut::new();
while let Some(chunk) = body_stream.next().await {
  buf.extend_from_slice(&chunk?);
  while let Some(pos) = find_double_newline(&buf) {
    let event = buf.split_to(pos + 2);
    let payload = extract_data_lines(&event);
    if let Ok(v) = serde_json::from_slice::<Value>(&payload) {
      handle_event(v);
    } else {
      // log and drop or push to retry queue
    }
  }
}
```

jordan: also check reconnect/auth handling — if we recreate the request on reconnect be careful to preserve the auth header (token) and backoff so we don't hammer. For retries use exponential backoff + jitter.

sam: good call. Right now reconnects are aggressive in dev build. I'll add jitter.

rusty: on backpressure — if event processing is slow you need a bounded buffer so you don't OOM. I usually pair a bounded `tokio::sync::mpsc::channel(bound)` with a worker pool. If the channel is full, pause reading from the HTTP body (i.e., stop polling the stream) — in async streams that means simply await on the send and that will naturally apply backpressure.

miguel: if using `reqwest` body stream + `StreamExt::next()`, you control the loop and can `await` on sending into the bounded mpsc; that effectively stalls consumption when processing lags.

anya: extra tip: SSE sometimes uses multiple `data:` lines for one event. Concatenate them in order, not line-by-line parse. Also handle `data: [DONE]` sentinel if the API sends it.

sam: any objections if I add a small util in the SDK: `SseEventParser` that yields complete JSON values and is tested against partial-chunk fixtures? I'll open a PR. :eyes:

ci-bot: CI status for branch sam/sse-rework: running — 3 tests queued, coverage job scheduled.

jordan: pinging about auth edge-case: if token refresh happens mid-stream we should surface error and let the app decide to reconnect. Don't silently refresh inside the stream handler.

rusty: +1. Simpler to fail fast and let upper layer handle token rotation. Adds clarity.

sam: cool. I'll: 1) implement parser util (incremental reassemble + serde deserializer fallback), 2) add bounded mpsc with worker pool to apply backpressure, 3) add jittered reconnect and fail-on-auth-expire. I'll link PR in an hour for review.

anya: I'll review that PR this afternoon. :) :thumbsup:

sam: thanks — tagging @anya and @miguel for review when ready.

miguel: sounds good. also add a small integration test that simulates chunk boundaries like `"{\"k\":1"` then `"23}\n\n"` to catch regressions.

sam: will do — thanks everyone.
